<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tibeto-burman-languages">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tibeto-Burman languages</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tibeto-Burman is the name for the large group of Sino-Tibetan languages other than Chinese, spoken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> to Myanmar and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It includes Tibetan and Burmese, both with long literary traditions, hundreds of smaller languages, and extinct languages known from texts, such as Tangut and the language of Zhangzhung.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tibeto-burman-languages.jpg" alt="Tibeto-Burman languages" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fobos92 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lenguas_tibeto-birmanas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Tibeto-Burman languages are the non-Sinitic members of the Sino-Tibetan family. More than 400 are spoken across the Southeast Asian Massif and parts of East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, by around 60 million people. The family takes its name from its two most widely spoken members, Burmese and the Tibetic languages, whose literary traditions date from the 12th and 7th centuries respectively. Most of the other languages are spoken by much smaller communities, many of them in remote mountain areas, and many have not been described in detail or lack a written standard.</p><p>In the south the Karen languages are spoken by three million people on both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> border. Burmese, the national language of Myanmar, has over 32 million speakers and belongs to the Lolo-Burmese group of about 100 languages. More than eight million people on the Tibetan Plateau and in neighbouring areas speak Tibetic languages, and Classical Tibetan has an extensive literature dating from the 8th century. The Newar language of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> has a literature dating from the 12th century, and Meithei is the main language of Manipur. The widest variety lies in the highlands from northern Myanmar to northeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Two historical languages, Pyu and Tangut, are believed to be Tibeto-Burman, though their exact affiliation is uncertain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In the 18th century scholars noticed parallels between Tibetan and Burmese. In the next century Brian Houghton Hodgson collected data on the non-literary languages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> and northeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and noted that many were related to them. James Logan first applied the name Tibeto-Burman in 1856 and added Karen in 1858. Julius Klaproth had noted in 1823 that Burmese, Tibetan and Chinese share basic vocabulary, and Jean Przyluski introduced the term Sino-Tibetan in 1924. The link between Tibeto-Burman and Chinese is now accepted by most linguists.</p><p>The classification remains disputed. Paul Benedict, in a work of 1972 written around 1941, saw Chinese as the first branch to split off, and James Matisoff later modified his scheme. Shafer's classification of 1955 did not recognise Tibeto-Burman at all. Some historical linguists criticise the split into Sinitic and Tibeto-Burman because the non-Sinitic languages share no innovations in phonology or morphology that would show them to form a branch. George van Driem rejects it and makes Tibeto-Burman a synonym of Sino-Tibetan. New languages continue to be recognised, some not closely related to others, among them languages described only in the 2010s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tibeto-Burman languages” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bon texts present Zhangzhung as the land where the teacher Tonpa Shenrab's religion first flourished, and they preserve words of its <b>Tibeto-Burman</b> language.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhangzhung">Zhangzhung</a></li></ul></section>
